other_moves.detail:
Franchise on Do Nothing:
- operating profit 5.2 (2050), 4.4 (2055) and 3.6 (2060, widebody only);
- narrowbody profit falls to zero by 2060;
- net debt $0.0B in base and stress through 2060;
- Malave's caveat: the tracker holds free cash flow flat, and the run-down passed through takes about $1.7B a year by 2060 (stress FCF about $7.5B).
The proposal has four parts:
(a) net debt at or below $0 in the stress case is the first claim, and no return is ever debt-funded;
(b) the MAX tail, fleet support and the 787 and 777X lines are funded first;
(c) the keep-warm cost: no figure goes out before the planning cycle [BX-0549];
(d) the capacity to fund a 10-year fps from cash flow is kept (peak about $8.4B), so returns come only from surplus and stay flexible.
Reported plainly: a launch after 2050 is a cold start. Nothing on returns goes public until the Board decides.

Pope's ranking, with Malave's stop list:
(1) fleet support, for life;
(2) type data, production-system records and tooling for spares, permanently, at low cost;
(3) the Renton site, held and not sold, to a 2055 review;
(4) engine technology talks with CFM and the Joint Venture, at no cost and with no commitment;
(5) a core design and technology team, bounded, to a 2055 review;
(6) 737 suppliers kept hot only to the last MAX in 2059.
We stop paying for the Renton conversion plan, a launch-scale programme office and engine capacity-slot talks. Items (5) and (6) stop at their review dates unless a business case exists. Every $0.1B a year kept warm is about 0.7% of base and 1.1% of stress free cash flow.

memo_weighing.cfo:
Weighed in full, and decisive on the fps. His buffer veto binds on all 12 fps rows. I accept it with no override, and I'd not seek one. His estimates have earned weight [BX-1316]: his 2045 forecast for this row (about -2.2, range -1.9 to -2.6) landed at -2.30, and his cost estimate held at $2.56B.

I took the following from his memo:
- the reset arithmetic: one two-year slip widens the miss by $0.15-0.65B, and the board's 'free slip' is an artefact of booking at EIS that I don't bank;
- Fundable when the 777X turns: a pass on the 10-year rows and the Re-engine; a fail on the cash for the 7-year at new sites, the 7-year with the Re-engine and via Embraer;
- the cash path: every launch lands on the MAX run-down, and the 10-year's 2056 peak leaves about $1.4B of stress headroom;
- his 20% A350 Re-engine number (15-25%) for my expected column;
- the premium log: $0.45B in S1, $0.24B weighted;
- the four-part capital-allocation proposal for the Finance Committee, with no keep-warm figure published before the planning cycle.

Thin seat: his record is one call (Low), so beyond the buffer test his views are inference or doctrine and I weigh them as advice. This round the advice and the grid agree.

memo_weighing.ops:
Weighed as the doctrine seat it is. Her airplane-business evidence is Very low, and her own record is Global Services, as she says.

Her KPI result was confirmed by a search of the brief, not assumed. I carry her caveat to the Board: the board has no measured KPI, supplier or workforce data.

I adopted:
- her written step-down plan as the Aerospace Safety Committee report: one gated step a year, three line moves in ten years, suppliers hot to the last unit, LEAP-1B secured privately, critical skills retained, and the order book closed at the glide path;
- her disclosure flags: 'a pace we can sustain safely', no 'supply base ready', and 'Any new single-aisle airplane we launch';
- her keep-warm ranking, with review dates;
- new_site while the fps holds.

I confirmed her strain counts (0 / 2 / 5). Her Re-engine readiness fail (engine not committed, a single source) is an added reason for hold, not the deciding one. Her ramp comparison (the 10-year is the predictable ramp) and her finding that via Embraer brings nothing we lack match the grid.

Her site inferences (Everett North released first, the last line at Renton) are my planning basis, subject to KPI data, not yet decided.

