recommendations.text:
The Aerospace Safety Committee asks for Pope's written KPI report on the MAX step-down as it begins. From 2050 capacity binds: 437 aircraft wanted against 428 built in 2050, 365 against 299 in 2053 and 305 against 214 in 2055. The line must not be stretched to meet that demand. We expect the following:
- each step down KPI-gated and agreed with the FAA;
- lines consolidated one at a time, and never during a rate break;
- fragile suppliers kept funded and hot, with last-time buys and tooling kept for spares;
- LEAP-1B volumes and spares secured for our own lines;
- trained people retained;
- support for the in-service MAX fleet kept whole for its life.
Weekly safety-review risks should keep reaching us. Any development plan must have no production-certification concurrency and no fixed-price development.

We are a long-cycle business. Bring us the written franchise view on the 2050 grid, and with it the capital-allocation question that is reserved to this Board. On Do Nothing, net debt has been $0.0B in the base case since 2029 and in the stress case since 2030. It holds through 2060, with free cash flow of about $13.8B a year, and no development is funded. Our Finance Committee will take up the question of returns to the owners. It asks Malave for a proposal that does four things:
- holds net debt at the historical level in the stress case;
- funds the MAX tail, fleet support and the widebody lines first;
- states the off-grid cost of keeping the fps option warm (design team, suppliers, engine talks, the Renton plan);
- keeps the capacity to fund a future airplane from cash flow.
A 10-year launch after 2050 would meet the MAX at zero output, so any later decision would be a cold start. Report that plainly. Nothing on returns goes public until this Board decides.

The public statement and the package.

The statement:
- no fps date, and no programme announced ahead of this Board;
- no Airbus move given as the reason for any order;
- MAX rates stepped down on the data and agreed with the FAA;
- nothing on cash returns.
Now that capacity binds, we prefer Pope's wording, 'at a pace we can sustain safely, stepping down on the data', to 'the pace our customers need', which promises output the line will not build.

The package should show:
- the submitted row and each item row across S1-S3;
- S3 against Do Nothing (floor -2.28);
- Malave's buffer, stress net-debt and Fundable results;
- Pope's KPI, supplier and strain results in writing;
- the status of H3's second half and the H4 overlap counts;
- every premium logged against H8's $2B cap.

frame.question:
It's 2050, the last decision year on this board. NGSA has been in service for thirteen years. Our narrowbody preference is 26% and falling. From this year the MAX phase-out binds: customers want 437 from us against 428 we can build, 305 against 214 in 2055, and from 2060 we build none. There is no later round, so whatever we order now is the board's final answer.

Three questions.
(1) Does anything on this grid clear the bar we set in 2045 for this round? The bar:
- more than $1B ahead of Do Nothing in the expected column;
- S3 within $2B of Do Nothing (floor -2.28);
- stress net debt at $0.0B;
- overlap of 2 years or less;
- no item below -$1B in any plausible column;
- Pope's readiness results.
This is the fps's fourth test against the three work streams, and the 787 Re-engine's last window.
(2) If nothing clears, will we say plainly what a hold means this time? On this board it is not a deferral. It means no new single-aisle airplane within the game. The MAX runs out in 2060 and the widebody and services carry the company. Any later launch is a cold start.
(3) How do we run the MAX step-down, which starts in earnest now, so that quality and the in-service fleet come first and customers are promised nothing the line can't build?

