frame.asks_cfo:
Jay, you're my independent check on the estimates [BX-1316]. Last round you put the 2050 10-year at about -2.2 (range -1.9 to -2.6). The brief says -2.30, so that was a good call. Please bring me the following.

(1) The buffer test on rows A-H, with S3 against Do Nothing's -0.28 (floor -2.28). My reading:
- B passes at +0.17, clear by 2.45.
- Every fps row fails:
  - D: -2.30, short by 0.02;
  - 10-year at new sites with the Re-engine: -2.40, short by 0.12;
  - C: -2.60, short by 0.32;
  - 10-year at new sites: -2.70, short by 0.42;
  - E: -3.56, short by 1.28;
  - 7-year at new sites: -3.69, short by 1.41;
  - G: -4.86 to -5.15, short by 2.58-2.87.
D misses by two hundredths. I'm not asking you to round it into a pass: the threshold is the threshold, and your veto binds on it. Confirm the miss on the brief's numbers, and tell me whether an honest reset narrows it or widens it.

(2) Each Board item against the same row with that field at its default, in S1 / S2 / S3:
- Re-engine alone: +0.45 / -0.59 / +0.45;
- Re-engine beside a 10-year: +0.30 / -0.74 / +0.30;
- Re-engine beside a 7-year: +0.17-0.18 / -0.87 / +0.17-0.18;
- Re-engine beside via Embraer: +0.19 / -0.85 to -0.86 / +0.19;
- 10-year at Renton against hold: -2.30 / -2.30 / -2.32 (new sites -2.40 / -2.40 / -2.42); beside the Re-engine, -2.45 / -2.45 / -2.47;
- 7-year at Renton: -3.24 / -3.24 / -3.28;
- via Embraer at Renton: -4.75 / -4.75 / -4.77;
- Renton against new sites at a launch: +0.10 on the 10-year, +0.13 to +0.14 on the 7-year.

(3) Stress net debt from the objective table. I read:
- $0.0B for A, B, every 10-year row and the 7-year at Renton alone;
- $0.7B for the 7-year at new sites;
- $2.0-3.1B for the 7-year with the Re-engine;
- $1.5-6.3B for via Embraer.

(4) Debt penalty: the grid already nets it, so never subtract it again. Quote the undiscounted scale only: Re-engine $0.6B, 10-year $19.1B, 7-year $22.0B, via Embraer $52.5B.

(5) Cash timing on the standard curve, and your 'Fundable when the 777X turns' result for each 2050 bill:
- Re-engine: $5B over 2050-54, booked in 2055;
- 10-year: $60.32B over 2050-59, booked in 2060;
- 7-year: $65.00B over 2050-56, booked in 2057;
- via Embraer: $100B over 2050-59.
Does any of them land on top of the cash the MAX run-down takes away?

(6) The slip leg, one conservative reset [BX-1318, BX-1319]:
- the Re-engine one and two years late (EIS 2056-57);
- the 10-year two years late (EIS 2062). The MAX builds nothing from 2060, so a late fps means years with no narrowbody output at all. Cost that once, in full.

(7) Your number for a same-round A350 Re-engine (S2). Last round you said about 20% (range 15-25%). Airbus's line is unchanged ('we continue to assess its evolution'), and Rolls-Royce has still launched no widebody engine. An A350 Re-engine costs Do Nothing only 0.10 this round. The Re-engine item breaks even at about 43%, so your number sizes the premium and sets my expected column; it doesn't decide the order.

(8) The premium, measured against the best grid plan in the expected column, not against Do Nothing:
- holding the Re-engine by my own choice: $0.45B on S1, about $0.24B weighted at 20%;
- objective premium: the cheapest fps is $2.30B below Do Nothing and $2.54B below the best weighted plan, and D is $2.45B below. All are beyond H8's $2B.
Confirm that no 2050 order moves any scored objective: the last scored year is 2050, the earliest fps EIS is 2057, and every plan shows one engine source in 2050.

(9) The end of the board. There is no later round, so the waiting case you made in 2045 has run out of road on the board. Confirm that the board counts 20 years from EIS (a 2060 EIS counts to 2079). If so, the grid prices the future airplane in full, and a hold is not an artefact of truncation.

(10) For the Finance Committee (Board recommendation 5), a capital-allocation proposal that:
- holds net debt at the historical level in the stress case;
- funds the MAX tail, fleet support and the widebody lines first;
- states the off-grid cost of keeping the fps option warm (design team, suppliers, engine talks, the Renton plan), and what we stop paying for if we don't;
- keeps the capacity to fund a future airplane from cash flow.
With it, bring the franchise numbers: narrowbody profit to zero by 2060, the widebody at $3.6B a year, net debt at $0.0B through 2060, and your caveat that the tracker holds free cash flow flat. Nothing on returns goes public until the Board decides.

Board risk: it says it would veto re787: launch on the Business case, and every fps launch. Your numbers are the case it will judge [BG-0022].

frame.asks_ops:
Stephanie (your seat runs on the operations doctrine), please bring me the following in writing.

(1) KPI status, confirmed from the brief, not assumed. Is any quality, FAA, certification or supply-chain problem live? I read none [BX-1271]. The Board takes nothing without your result.

(2) The MAX step-down KPI report for the Board's Aerospace Safety Committee [BG-0043]. This is the first year capacity binds.
- Capacity: 428 (2050), 385, 342, 299 (2053), 256, 214 (2055), 171, 128, 86, 43, then 0 (2060).
- Wanted: 437 (2050), 365 (2053) and 305 (2055).
The Board asks for the following, and so do I:
- each step down KPI-gated and agreed with the FAA;
- lines consolidated one at a time, never during a rate break;
- fragile suppliers kept funded and hot, with last-time buys and tooling kept for spares [B-2330, BX-1243];
- LEAP-1B volumes and spares secured for our own lines;
- trained people retained, and where they go;
- the in-service fleet supported whole for its life [BX-1251, BX-1273];
- the last customers told plainly and early: firm orders first, nothing promised beyond the glide path, and the line not stretched [BX-1274].

(3) Public wording. I'm taking your 2045 flag: 'at a pace we can sustain safely, stepping down on the data' replaces 'the pace our customers need'. Tell me if anything else in the line promises output we won't build.

(4) Two-front strain counts for 2050 launches. My reading:
- Re-engine alone: 0;
- beside a 10-year fps or via Embraer: 2 (2053-55);
- beside a 7-year: 5 (2050-55).

(5) Re-engine readiness, for the record (my lean is hold):
- a widebody engine committed and ready by a 2055 EIS (the GEnx upgrade has been ready since 2033; Rolls-Royce has launched no widebody engine);
- the amended type certificate before the line converts, with no concurrency [BX-1263];
- Charleston stable [BX-1317].

(6) The ramps are within epsilon for the first time: the 7-year trails the 10-year by 0.94-0.99. In 2045 you said the 7-year's warmer handover should count only if that happened. Give me the operational comparison:
- the 7-year (EIS 2057) meets the MAX at 128 a year;
- the 10-year (EIS 2060) meets it at zero, after 23 years without a major development.
Which can operations deliver predictably, and what would a slip do when the MAX line is already cold? This is for the record only: both ramps fail on value.

(7) Engine readiness. Codes 5, 6 and 7 give CFM plus the Joint Venture at a 2057 or 2060 EIS. Are those two real sources or two on paper?

(8) Solo or via Embraer on IP [BX-1330]. Via Embraer is $2.45B below the Solo 10-year in every column. Does it bring anything we lack?

(9) Renton. Is there any operational reason to move fps_site while the fps holds? On the glide path, Renton's lines free up by the mid-2050s.

(10) The operations side of the franchise view. If we hold, on this board Boeing builds no narrowbody after 2059. What does that mean for people, suppliers, the Renton site and services? Now that any later launch is a cold start, what is worth keeping warm, at what off-grid cost, and for how long? I want the honest answer, not the comfortable one.

Board risk: it vetoes any launch without your written KPI, supplier and strain results, and any Re-engine without a 2055 engine committed and the type certificate before conversion.

