tests.numbers:
The brief reports no problem (test 1).
Glide path from the brief (a year / a month / against 2043's 684):
- 2050: 428 / 35.7 / 63%;
- 2051-52: 385 / 32.1 and 342 / 28.5;
- 2053: 299 / 24.9 / 44%;
- 2054: 256 / 21.3;
- 2055: 214 / 17.8 / 31%;
- 2056-58: 171 / 14.2, 128 / 10.7 and 86 / 7.2;
- 2059: 43 / 3.6 / 6%;
- 2060: 0.
That leaves 2,352 airplanes to build.

(a) Steps. Capacity falls about 3.6 a month each year, below the 5-a-month step size, so it is one step down a year. Each step is held until KPIs at the new rate are stable (2-3 months [BX-1271]). Breaks are at least 6 months apart [B-2351], and each step is agreed with the FAA [B-2236].

(b) Lines, at about 15.7 a month a line (Renton's 47 over three):
- three lines in 2050-51;
- two from 2052 (28.5 a month);
- one from 2056 (14.2, which fits one 15-a-month line);
- none from 2060.
That is three moves in ten years, each in a half-year with no rate break [B-1446]. My inference on sequence: release Everett North first, if it still builds the MAX, which frees floor next to the widebody lines. Keep the last line at Renton, with the 737's home team, to 2059. This is a site call for the CEO, and it is not priced.

(c) Suppliers. Volume against 2043 is 63% (2050), 31% (2055), 19% (2057) and 6% (2059).
- Fragile 737-unique suppliers are funded and paced with buffers through the last unit [B-2330, BX-1243, B-2043].
- Last-time buys are placed before each one exits.
- Tooling and qualifications are kept for spares for the fleet's life.
- Suppliers shared with the 787 and 777X carry on.

(d) LEAP-1B. 4,704 original-equipment engines over 2050-59 (856 in 2050, 428 in 2055, 86 in 2059), plus fleet spares.
- Secure the yearly volumes privately now.
- Confirm the engines for every unit before build, without drawing on fleet spares.
- CFM's public rule gives kits and spares first call, so the last builds must be booked early.
- Materials are for our own lines and attributed to no one.

(e) People. Output halves by 2055 (214 against 428) and ends in 2060. The end of a line is a quality risk, because skills leave before the work does. So we retain the critical skills (inspection, quality and final assembly) to the last unit in 2059. Where they go:
- the widebody lines are flat at 100 a year (8.3 a month) on Do Nothing, so they absorb only attrition;
- fleet support and services grow with the fleet;
- a core design team stays, bounded (test 13).
Everyone else is told early, on the step-down schedule. Most MAX production jobs end by 2060, and I won't say otherwise.

(f) Fleet. 15,746 MAX were delivered on the board in 2026-59, plus today's fleet. All are supported whole for their life [BX-1251, BX-1273]. This is unpriced.

(g) Customers. Wanted against built:
- 437 against 428 in 2050 (9 short);
- 365 against 299 in 2053 (66 short);
- 305 against 214 in 2055 (91 short);
- 137 against 0 in 2060.
The order book closes at the glide path: no slot is sold beyond capacity. Orders already placed are served first, then waiting customers in sequence. Customers are told plainly and early, year by year, and the line is not stretched [BX-1274]. Risks are reported weekly to the Committee [BG-0043].

Limit: the board carries no KPI, supplier or workforce data, so this is a plan with triggers, not a measurement.

rate_737 offers only hold. Demand from us exceeds capacity from 2050 (437 against 428), but:
- no lever adds MAX capacity;
- the phase-out binds the status quo too;
- visible demand for us is falling (preference 26% in 2050 and 16% in 2060; replacement 966 in 2050 and 553 in 2060).
I would not add capacity for the tail even if a lever existed.

The honest answer: on a hold, Boeing builds no narrowbody after 2059 on this board. Any later launch is a cold start: a new production system, a re-qualified supply base and a rebuilt engineering team. With both the fps and the Re-engine held, Boeing does no major development on this board after the 777X; by 2060 that is 33 years.
- People: MAX output halves by 2055 and ends in 2060. Widebody output is flat at 100 a year, and services grow with the fleet.
- Suppliers: 737-unique volume falls to 6% of 2043 by 2059. Many will exit after the last-time buys. The suppliers shared with the 787 and 777X stay.
- Renton: free by 2060.
- Services: 15,746 MAX delivered on the board in 2026-59, plus today's fleet, need parts, modifications, data and training for decades. That is Boeing IP, so it is where we invest [BX-1330, BX-1329]. It is unpriced.

What to keep warm, ranked by IP and cost:
(1) fleet support, for life;
(2) the type data, production-system records and tooling for spares, permanently, at low cost;
(3) the Renton site, held and not sold, to a review in 2055;
(4) engine talks, at no cost;
(5) a core design and technology team, bounded to a 2055 review. A team without a programme decays, and a 2055 decision already means a 2062-65 EIS, 2-5 years after the MAX ends;
(6) 737 suppliers kept hot only to the last MAX in 2059, not beyond, because nothing on this board funds them after it.

Off-grid cost: the brief has no number, so I won't invent one; Malave owns it. Sensitivity: every $0.1B a year kept warm is 0.72% of base and 1.09% of stress free cash flow. Stop paying for items (5) and (6) at their review dates unless a business case exists.

