frame.question:
What game are we playing, and how do we win? [CX-0467]

This is the last round, and the game is the installed base we already have. No new slot is open on this board.
- NGSA has been in service since 2037 on code 7, with all three makers fitted.
- Our Ducted (ready 2041), Embraer (counting since 2042) and GEnx upgrade (counting since 2042) can only hold.
- Boeing has not launched the fps. An fps launched now enters service in 2057 on a 7-year plan or 2060 on a 10-year plan, both after the board closes the narrowbody window in 2056. No airframe left on this board can pick us or drop us.

One thing is new, and I want it tested hard rather than waved through. The Open Fan's cost against hold, $13.72B alone or $13.04B with Lobby, now fits under the $15B RISE allowance for the first time. It's the last round, and that will tempt us. On the grid it buys nothing:
- a 2050 launch is ready in 2060;
- NGSA has flown for thirteen years;
- the window closes in 2056;
- 'ready by 2050' can't be met;
- no airframer has asked for it.
That's the installed-base lens talking [CX-0555], and I name it.

So we win by closing out well, in this order:
- safety and durability of the LEAP-1A, LEAP-1B, Ducted and GEnx fleets as the 737 MAX steps down to none by 2060 and the last A320 family aircraft leave the lines;
- kits and spares first;
- RISE kept whole as technology with Safran;
- the balance sheet and the payout untouched;
- a straight account to the Board.

"We get paid in dollars, not in percentages" [CX-0630]. I'll tell you what we know and tell you what we don't [CX-0436].

frame.asks_cfo:
Rahul, this is the last round, and I need you to push on me harder than usual. The last round is where people talk themselves into things.

(1) Rows. Against hold, S1 / S2, give me the expected column, the S1 cost, the worst column and the premium in dollars. Check my numbers:
- Open Fan alone: -13.72 / -13.72, which is the $8B bill plus $5.72B.
- Open Fan + Lobby: -13.04 / -13.04, which is the $9B bill plus $4.04B.
- Lobby next to the Open Fan alone: +0.68 / +0.68.
- Lobby alone is off the grid; I read it at -1.00 in each column.
Rebuild the $5.72B the way you rebuilt the $11.22B: every CFM engine on NGSA lost from 2050 through 2056. Tell me if anything else is in it.

(2) Strain. Confirm the $2B is already booked and that an Open Fan adds none. If you read a new $2B, tell me, and the numbers move.

(3) Pays for itself and the payout floor. Confirm every Open Fan row fails against hold. The Open Fan is $0.8B a year over 2050-2060, or $0.9B with Lobby, against about $2.5B of room after returning at least 70% of free cash flow. Confirm the floor isn't the binding test.

(4) Premium and the RISE allowance, the one new point. $13.72B and $13.04B now fit under $15B. Tell me plainly whether anything in your tests opens the allowance:
- there is no airframer path, and the wait applies in every column;
- a 2060 ready year earns nothing inside a window that closes in 2056;
- 'ready by 2050' can't be met, so no objective premium attaches.
Hold is $0.0B, and the game total is $0.0B of $4.0B.

(5) Weights. Provisional S1 0.70 / S2 0.30, your set from the last two rounds. Boeing gives no date for the fourth round, but its MAX runs out by 2060. Put your own weights on it. The columns are identical, so it doesn't move the call.

(6) Post-mortem on the 2045 booking (a Board ask), one line. We booked -25.0, and hold stayed at -24.41. The $0.59B provision went undrawn because no named move came. Score it. Was the method right, or did we over-provision?

(7) Un-gridded risks this round, sized and weighted, named moves only:
(a) a Trent 1000 upgrade counting from 2053 (your 2045 figure: -$0.41B for a 2050 launch);
(b) UltraFan WB behind a re-engine (-$0.50B from 2056, exclusive with (a));
(c) an A350 re-engine entering in 2055, which shifts WB airframe share away from the 787. My rough figure is -$0.27B on GEnx; Airbus says it is still assessing;
(d) a 787 re-engine and whether it touches the GEnx fit gain;
(e) confirm that any fps launched now enters after 2056 and has no NB effect.
Recommend a booking at or below -24.41. My provisional is about -24.6, with a named downside of about -25.2.

(8) Objective. Confirm 45.6% in 2050 at hold. Confirm the total-share cut from an Open Fan launched now (your 2045 estimate was about 20 points, or 14 with Lobby), and that 'ready by 2050' can't be met. No objective premium.

(9) Terms. Ducted and LEAP go to both airframers, including any new Boeing single-aisle, on standard terms, with no launch-era pricing and no concession to win a code [CX-0930, CX-0609]. NB price and capacity stay joint with Safran [CX-0184]. The LEAP-1B and LEAP-1A spares tail is contracted with Safran against shop-visit forecasts, with no cost booked until those volumes are agreed. At least 70% of free cash flow is returned, and the A-range rating is untouched [CX-0645, CX-0252].

BOARD GUIDANCE (non-binding). The Board wants three things from you in numbers: your pays-for-itself test (failing for both Open Fan rows), R&D against the payout floor, and confirmation that the $2B strain is already booked. It would veto:
- the Open Fan alone;
- the Open Fan with Lobby;
- Lobby alone;
- any premium over $2.0B;
- any RISE allowance this round, because no airframer path exists;
- any plan with one of your binding vetoes standing.

