board_guidance.expect_to_see:
Both fields offer only `hold`, so this package will not reach a Board review. This is the last decision year, so this guidance is the Board's final word in the game. The Board expects the ExCo record (rationale and sealed orders) to contain the following.

1. Grid. The hold/hold row: -2.16 in S1 (rivals hold) and -2.16 in S2 (Boeing launches fps 10-yr, code 7). A plain statement that no Board item exists. The record should explain why S2 moved from -2.17 (2045) to -2.16: an fps launched now enters service in 2060, outside the board's narrowbody window to 2056.

2. From the CEO:
- his expected scenario;
- `predictions` over S1 and S2, with `expected_pv_b` of -2.16 on any weights;
- `premium_b` of 0 and an empty `overrides`;
- a `board_response` to each recommendation below.
All 2045 recommendations were answered in 2045. What falls due now are the 2045 commitments: the reset check, the capacity case, the Joint Venture terms to Finance, the annual durability report and the annual NPV review.

3. From Mitchill:
- one governing set of weights;
- confirmation of $0 R&D in 2050, $2.00B total R&D (P&W's Joint Venture share only) and no write-off;
- the forecast check from 2045. The Board reads the brief's 2045-49 rows as identical to the 2045 forecast: 830 / 866 / 861 / 887 / 925 engines, and values 7.5 / 8.0 / 8.1 / 8.6 / 9.1 against status quo 7.2 / 7.8 / 7.9 / 8.4 / 9.1. On the rounded table that is about +0.13 PV realised in 2045-49, so +0.27 of the +0.41 PV for 2041-56 is now realised and about +0.14 remains forecast for 2050-56. The Board computed this from the rounded table, whose own sum gives about -0.11 against the brief's -0.16 engine value. The brief's figures govern, and Mitchill should confirm with his own;
