would_veto:
For the record, these orders are not offered and should not be submitted (a field left out reads as `hold`):
- `jv_with_rr: withdraw`: the Joint Venture formed in 2035, and the rules allow withdrawal only before formation;
- `jv_with_rr: commit`: P&W is already committed;
- `gtf2_solo: cancel`, `launch` or `launch_if_selected`: GTF2 folded in 2035, and the Joint Venture engine has been ready since 2036.
Were any of them ever offered, a new launch would face Committed airframe and Bounded downside, and an exit would face Premature exit, because P&W is fitted on a live airframe [P-1342, P-1191, PG-0019, P-0746].

Other moves and the public statement draw recommendations only. The Board would press hard against, but cannot veto:
- a Capitalise claim or payback figure in public;
- a time-on-wing or durability figure beyond proof;
- any word on Boeing's dates, sources or code;
- any launch-style discount or concession for the fps;
- permanent capacity sized for the 2052 peak without a case against a buyback [PG-0039, PG-0050, PX-0055, P-1165].

recommendations.text:
Capitalise, final account: say it plainly. Booked -2.16, unchanged from 2045: engine value -0.16, R&D -2.00. The 2045 reset trigger has not fired:
- the 2045-49 rows match the 2045 forecast line for line;
- of the +0.41 PV recovery for 2041-56, about +0.27 is realised (2041-49) and about +0.14 remains forecast (2050-56), per the Board's reading of the rounded table, to be confirmed by Mitchill;
- years after 2056 lie outside the board's window and must not be claimed.
Record the lesson from 2045: any engine a rival names in public is priced as the low end, even when no column carries it. Use no premium, discount or concession to chase the gap. Long-term shareowner value is now served by running the in-service engine well and returning cash in the Board's order.

Public statement. Keep the 2045 lines, which hold on the record: the shortfall owned without a figure, the fleet first, gear and core ours, Rolls-Royce speaking for itself, and the engine offered only where it is ready by entry into service. Then:
- update the share line to the record (about 26.7% of narrowbody engines in 2049);
- claim no Capitalise result, payback or return figure;
- publish no time-on-wing or durability figure beyond what is proven, and promise no block upgrade;
- say nothing on Boeing's or Airbus's dates, rates, sources or the A320 tail beyond P&W's own spares-first rule.

